  {"question": "According to arXiv 2510.26007, why is it necessary to evaluate both group and individual fairness in recommender systems, and what limitation exists if only one is used?", "answer": "Evaluating both is necessary because metrics for one granularity cannot estimate the other; using only one misses important fairness aspects.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "metrics for one granularity level cannot be used as a proxy to the other, i.e., group fairness metrics cannot estimate individual fairness [Rampisela et al., 2025b]."}, {"page": 4, "quote": "one should evaluate for both group and individual fairness to ensure that no groups or individuals are disadvantaged."}]},
  {"question": "What does arXiv 2510.26007 recommend regarding the use of redundant fairness metrics in recommender systems, and why?", "answer": "It recommends avoiding redundant metrics because some yield similar conclusions, so computing one may suffice.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "some metrics may be redundant as they yield similar conclusions to other existing metrics. As such, computing one of the similar metrics may suffice."}, {"page": 4, "quote": "the use of redundant (highly similar) measures should be avoided."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2510.26007, what is the purpose of min-max normalisation in corrected fairness metrics?", "answer": "Min-max normalisation ensures that 0 maps to the fairest case and 1 to the unfairest, making interpretation easier.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "We also applied min-max normalisation, such that the score 0 is correctly mapped to the fairest possible case and 1 to the unfairest possible case [Rampisela et al., 2024a, 2025a]."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2510.26007, what does 'AIS' stand for?", "answer": "AIS stands for 'AI in Science'.", "evidence": [{"page": 1, "quote": "The development of Artificial Intelligence (AI), including AI in Science (AIS), should be done following the principles of responsible AI."}]} ,
  {"question": "Which mathematical issue can make some fairness metrics in recommender systems unusable, as discussed in arXiv 2510.26007?", "answer": "Some metrics crash due to invalid operations like division by zero, making them unusable.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "some metrics would either crash upon computation due to invalid mathematical operations (e.g., division by 0), resulting in the metric outputting no scores that can be used for fairness assessment, rendering it unusable."}]} ,
  {"question": "In arXiv 2510.26007, what problem arises when a fairness metric's score range is narrower than its theoretical range?", "answer": "It causes difficulty interpreting scores, as actual outputs may not reach theoretical extremes, misleading fairness assessment.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "Suppose that in theory, a metric score ranges between 0 and 1, where 0 is the fairest score and 1 is the unfairest score. In some cases, there is no input to the metric that would output 0 or 1. Instead, for example, its score could only range between 0.3 and 0.6 (i.e., the minimum and maximum reachable scores, respectively). This causes difficulty in interpreting the metric score, as one would interpret a score of 0.5 as somehow fair if the range is [0, 1], but unfair if the range is [0.3, 0.6]."}]}
]}
